1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:02,910
A confident answer is cheap.

2
00:00:03,550 --> 00:00:04,570
A working change?

3
00:00:04,990 --> 00:00:05,810
That needs evidence.

4
00:00:06,690 --> 00:00:08,630
In this course, we're going to connect the

5
00:00:08,630 --> 00:00:11,730
code, the test, the pull request, and the

6
00:00:11,730 --> 00:00:13,330
machine that actually runs it.

7
00:00:13,890 --> 00:00:15,930
And when one of those pieces disagrees with

8
00:00:15,930 --> 00:00:18,650
the agent, well, that's where
the engineering starts.

9
00:00:21,150 --> 00:00:22,870
Welcome to Superposition.

10
00:00:23,670 --> 00:00:26,410
We're building an engineering
workflow in Cursor, from

11
00:00:26,410 --> 00:00:28,690
the first small change, all the way to

12
00:00:28,690 --> 00:00:31,130
a system with multiple services and agents.

13
00:00:31,850 --> 00:00:34,110
You'll learn the features,
sure, but the more

14
00:00:34,110 --> 00:00:36,850
useful skill is knowing what to ask, what

15
00:00:36,850 --> 00:00:39,210
to inspect, and when you
have enough evidence

16
00:00:39,210 --> 00:00:39,970
to move on.

17
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:44,590
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

18
00:00:44,590 --> 00:00:47,790
able to separate three things, a claim, an

19
00:00:47,790 --> 00:00:49,510
observation, and a conclusion.

20
00:00:50,290 --> 00:00:51,470
That sounds a little academic.

21
00:00:51,970 --> 00:00:52,410
It's not.

22
00:00:52,850 --> 00:00:55,410
It's the difference between
an agent saying, done,

23
00:00:55,790 --> 00:00:58,590
and you knowing which
requirement was actually met.

24
00:00:59,230 --> 00:01:00,730
Let's start with something we can inspect.

25
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:06,390
This is Operations Hub,
our starting service.

26
00:01:07,070 --> 00:01:10,390
Right now, it's deliberately
small, an API, a

27
00:01:10,390 --> 00:01:13,510
database, and synthetic requests
from two organizations.

28
00:01:14,330 --> 00:01:16,630
You're looking at real data returned by the

29
00:01:16,630 --> 00:01:17,290
running service.

30
00:01:17,850 --> 00:01:19,890
This is the foundation we will grow during

31
00:01:19,890 --> 00:01:22,770
the course, not a finished
application hiding behind

32
00:01:22,770 --> 00:01:23,450
a demo screen.

33
00:01:25,917 --> 00:01:29,017
Notice the title, the
status, and the organization

34
00:01:29,017 --> 00:01:30,877
identifier on each request.

35
00:01:31,517 --> 00:01:33,997
A laptop approval belongs
to one organization.

36
00:01:34,557 --> 00:01:36,497
A training request belongs to another.

37
00:01:37,377 --> 00:01:40,677
Later, that organization boundary
becomes a serious design

38
00:01:40,677 --> 00:01:41,197
constraint.

39
00:01:41,817 --> 00:01:43,977
A user from one company must not be

40
00:01:43,977 --> 00:01:45,897
able to read another company's requests.

41
00:01:48,483 --> 00:01:50,743
And here's our first useful distinction.

42
00:01:51,383 --> 00:01:54,303
Seeing two different organization
identifiers does not prove

43
00:01:54,303 --> 00:01:57,283
that access is isolated,
it only proves those

44
00:01:57,283 --> 00:01:59,063
values came back in this response.

45
00:01:59,703 --> 00:02:03,563
To establish isolation we'll need
identities, server-side permission

46
00:02:03,563 --> 00:02:06,623
checks, and tests that
attempt access across the

47
00:02:06,623 --> 00:02:06,943
boundary.

48
00:02:07,483 --> 00:02:09,883
The conclusion has to
stay inside the evidence.

49
00:02:12,250 --> 00:02:14,150
Now look at the health endpoint.

50
00:02:14,850 --> 00:02:17,350
The service reports that
its database is connected.

51
00:02:18,070 --> 00:02:20,710
In this baseline, the
handler actually sends a

52
00:02:20,710 --> 00:02:24,190
small query to PostgreSQL
before returning success.

53
00:02:24,930 --> 00:02:27,690
That's more informative than
a hardcoded green light,

54
00:02:28,090 --> 00:02:29,930
but it still doesn't tell us whether a

55
00:02:29,930 --> 00:02:32,010
manager can approve a request correctly.

56
00:02:34,583 --> 00:02:37,103
Let's connect that response to the code.

57
00:02:37,763 --> 00:02:40,603
Here's the route, here's
the database query, and

58
00:02:40,603 --> 00:02:42,183
here's the response it constructs.

59
00:02:42,943 --> 00:02:45,343
You don't need to memorize every symbol yet.

60
00:02:46,083 --> 00:02:47,223
Follow the cause and effect.

61
00:02:47,743 --> 00:02:49,983
An incoming request selects a handler.

62
00:02:50,503 --> 00:02:53,003
The handler talks to a dependency, and the

63
00:02:53,003 --> 00:02:54,023
caller gets a result.

64
00:02:56,583 --> 00:02:59,323
Cursor gives an agent tools to search, edit,

65
00:02:59,823 --> 00:03:01,463
and run commands around a model.

66
00:03:01,903 --> 00:03:04,043
That makes it powerful enough to do useful

67
00:03:04,043 --> 00:03:06,623
work, and powerful enough
to make a plausible

68
00:03:06,623 --> 00:03:07,163
mistake.

69
00:03:07,743 --> 00:03:09,223
Our job is to give it a clear

70
00:03:09,223 --> 00:03:12,403
objective, relevant
context, and a way to check

71
00:03:12,403 --> 00:03:12,963
the result.

72
00:03:13,503 --> 00:03:14,923
Then we inspect what happened.

73
00:03:17,250 --> 00:03:20,330
Here's a database integration
test from our baseline.

74
00:03:21,070 --> 00:03:23,830
It writes a synthetic record
inside a transaction,

75
00:03:24,110 --> 00:03:26,890
rolls the transaction
back, and checks that the

76
00:03:26,890 --> 00:03:27,790
record is absent.

77
00:03:28,470 --> 00:03:29,090
Watch the result.

78
00:03:29,670 --> 00:03:31,990
When that passes, we have evidence for the

79
00:03:31,990 --> 00:03:33,490
behavior this test exercises.

80
00:03:34,230 --> 00:03:36,530
We do not suddenly have evidence for every

81
00:03:36,530 --> 00:03:37,750
feature we plan to build.

82
00:03:40,217 --> 00:03:43,437
The same repository also has a real GitHub

83
00:03:43,437 --> 00:03:44,237
actions run.

84
00:03:44,897 --> 00:03:48,357
The workflow installs the locked
dependencies, initializes a

85
00:03:48,357 --> 00:03:51,337
fresh database, checks the
TypeScript, and runs the

86
00:03:51,337 --> 00:03:52,177
integration test.

87
00:03:52,957 --> 00:03:54,937
Running away from this desktop matters.

88
00:03:55,577 --> 00:03:57,317
It gives us a chance to catch assumptions

89
00:03:57,317 --> 00:03:59,157
that only happen to be true on the

90
00:03:59,157 --> 00:04:00,457
machine where we wrote the code.

91
00:04:02,950 --> 00:04:05,510
There's a detail here that's easy to skip.

92
00:04:06,150 --> 00:04:09,450
The commit.a passing check
belongs to a particular

93
00:04:09,450 --> 00:04:10,510
version of the code.

94
00:04:11,130 --> 00:04:13,050
If I change the code after that run,

95
00:04:13,550 --> 00:04:16,150
the old green badge doesn't verify the new

96
00:04:16,150 --> 00:04:16,490
change.

97
00:04:17,130 --> 00:04:19,370
So we'll keep connecting our evidence to the

98
00:04:19,370 --> 00:04:20,710
version it actually tested.

99
00:04:21,650 --> 00:04:22,230
Tiny habit,

100
00:04:22,950 --> 00:04:24,190
huge reduction in confusion.

101
00:04:26,817 --> 00:04:30,197
This pull request added our
Cloud startup configuration.

102
00:04:30,837 --> 00:04:32,937
Look at the files changed
rather than trusting

103
00:04:32,937 --> 00:04:33,437
the title.

104
00:04:34,177 --> 00:04:36,957
The configuration points to
an installation script and

105
00:04:36,957 --> 00:04:37,817
a startup script.

106
00:04:38,497 --> 00:04:40,677
The startup script brings
up the database and

107
00:04:40,677 --> 00:04:41,177
the API.

108
00:04:42,017 --> 00:04:44,817
We can inspect those
responsibilities before we accept

109
00:04:44,817 --> 00:04:46,157
the change into the main branch.

110
00:04:48,683 --> 00:04:50,923
During setup, we found a real problem in

111
00:04:50,923 --> 00:04:51,903
the final health check.

112
00:04:52,583 --> 00:04:55,503
If the API failed to become healthy, the

113
00:04:55,503 --> 00:04:57,863
script printed a warning and continued to a

114
00:04:57,863 --> 00:04:58,483
ready message.

115
00:04:59,203 --> 00:05:00,883
That's a success-shaped failure.

116
00:05:01,543 --> 00:05:03,943
The correction makes that
path return an error.

117
00:05:04,583 --> 00:05:06,223
We'll keep coming back to this idea.

118
00:05:07,083 --> 00:05:08,943
Failure needs to stay visible to the system

119
00:05:08,943 --> 00:05:09,923
coordinating the work.

120
00:05:12,217 --> 00:05:13,717
And here's the follow through.

121
00:05:14,177 --> 00:05:16,637
A normal cloud agent run on the merged

122
00:05:16,637 --> 00:05:17,077
version.

123
00:05:17,677 --> 00:05:19,897
Its job was only to inspect startup.

124
00:05:20,377 --> 00:05:23,657
No manual installation, no
manual service launch, and

125
00:05:23,657 --> 00:05:24,577
no code edits.

126
00:05:25,297 --> 00:05:27,937
The API responded, the
demo data was available,

127
00:05:28,337 --> 00:05:30,437
and PostgreSQL was accepting connections.

128
00:05:31,097 --> 00:05:32,957
The startup status was successful too.

129
00:05:35,350 --> 00:05:38,130
That tells us this starting
environment worked on

130
00:05:38,130 --> 00:05:38,570
this run.

131
00:05:39,170 --> 00:05:41,310
It doesn't promise that
every future change will

132
00:05:41,310 --> 00:05:41,570
work.

133
00:05:42,110 --> 00:05:45,650
A Cloud machine needs dependencies,
configuration, and running

134
00:05:45,650 --> 00:05:47,570
services, just like your laptop does.

135
00:05:48,370 --> 00:05:51,090
Later, we'll deliberately
break startup and learn to

136
00:05:51,090 --> 00:05:51,810
diagnose it.

137
00:05:52,230 --> 00:05:54,590
For now, notice that we're
checking the machine

138
00:05:54,590 --> 00:05:55,670
as well as the code.

139
00:05:58,017 --> 00:06:00,637
We'll grow three connected projects.

140
00:06:01,377 --> 00:06:04,317
Operations Hub becomes a business
application for requests

141
00:06:04,317 --> 00:06:04,997
and approvals.

142
00:06:05,797 --> 00:06:08,477
Evidence Desk becomes a
service that answers questions

143
00:06:08,477 --> 00:06:10,097
using authorized sources.

144
00:06:11,057 --> 00:06:13,857
Engineering Control Plane becomes
a system for coordinating

145
00:06:13,857 --> 00:06:14,897
engineering work.

146
00:06:15,517 --> 00:06:16,397
Those are the destinations.

147
00:06:17,177 --> 00:06:19,357
We'll build and verify
the capabilities step by

148
00:06:19,357 --> 00:06:22,557
step rather than pretending the
starting repositories already

149
00:06:22,557 --> 00:06:23,237
contain them.

150
00:06:25,483 --> 00:06:29,703
The complexity will come from
the behavior—permissions, state

151
00:06:29,703 --> 00:06:33,823
transitions, background work,
failures, and changes that cross

152
00:06:33,823 --> 00:06:34,863
a service boundary.

153
00:06:35,523 --> 00:06:38,423
Adding more agents won't
automatically make those problems

154
00:06:38,423 --> 00:06:38,943
easier.

155
00:06:39,643 --> 00:06:42,123
Sometimes the best design is one agent with

156
00:06:42,123 --> 00:06:43,163
a focused task.

157
00:06:43,763 --> 00:06:45,343
Sometimes parallel work helps.

158
00:06:45,783 --> 00:06:48,443
We'll make that decision from
dependencies and measured

159
00:06:48,443 --> 00:06:48,843
results.

160
00:06:51,250 --> 00:06:54,290
Our recurring workflow is
simple enough to remember.

161
00:06:55,390 --> 00:06:57,490
Understand the request, make a plan that can

162
00:06:57,490 --> 00:07:01,210
be checked, build a
bounded change, verify the

163
00:07:01,210 --> 00:07:04,290
behavior and inspect the
diff, then integrate it.

164
00:07:04,810 --> 00:07:06,990
If a check fails, return to the step

165
00:07:06,990 --> 00:07:08,210
that needs better information.

166
00:07:08,990 --> 00:07:11,150
A loop without a useful check is just

167
00:07:11,150 --> 00:07:12,690
repetition with a bigger bill.

168
00:07:15,183 --> 00:07:17,843
Your first checkpoint is a judgment call.

169
00:07:18,463 --> 00:07:21,243
An agent says, the approval
feature is complete.

170
00:07:21,723 --> 00:07:23,923
The server starts and the page looks right.

171
00:07:24,683 --> 00:07:27,383
Pause here, write down
two things that evidence

172
00:07:27,383 --> 00:07:30,243
supports and two things
it does not establish.

173
00:07:30,843 --> 00:07:33,403
Then choose one additional
check that would reduce

174
00:07:33,403 --> 00:07:34,943
the most important uncertainty.

175
00:07:42,350 --> 00:07:44,830
It supports that the server started in that

176
00:07:44,830 --> 00:07:47,370
environment and that the
observed page rendered.

177
00:07:47,990 --> 00:07:50,550
It doesn't establish that
approval permissions are enforced

178
00:07:50,550 --> 00:07:53,290
or that an approved request
is saved correctly.

179
00:07:53,930 --> 00:07:56,150
A useful next check could
attempt an approval

180
00:07:56,150 --> 00:07:58,930
as a user who lacks permission, then inspect

181
00:07:58,930 --> 00:08:01,030
both the response and the stored state.

182
00:08:01,590 --> 00:08:03,310
The best check depends on the requirement.

183
00:08:05,883 --> 00:08:07,783
That's the working
agreement for this course.

184
00:08:08,523 --> 00:08:10,883
We can use AI to move quickly while

185
00:08:10,883 --> 00:08:12,723
staying precise about what we know.

186
00:08:13,243 --> 00:08:15,863
Follow the request into
the system, connect checks

187
00:08:15,863 --> 00:08:18,463
to a specific version,
and keep the conclusion

188
00:08:18,463 --> 00:08:19,763
no bigger than the evidence.

189
00:08:20,643 --> 00:08:24,463
Next, we'll unpack the
model, context, tools, and

190
00:08:24,463 --> 00:08:26,783
runtime so you know which part to fix

191
00:08:26,783 --> 00:08:28,043
when an agent gets stuck.
